the Democratic Party. But I do think it's&nbsp;
important and interesting because Bernie also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:17.040
is an independent who has made very, very clear I&nbsp;
caucus with the Democrats and there are specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.080
reasons why Bernie is an independent. Vermont&nbsp;
is also a unique state in terms of its political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.940
orientation and alignment. Vermont Republicans&nbsp;
are different than national Republicans, and gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.420
ownership is a different thing. In Vermont,&nbsp;
we've talked about sort of the interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.420
different dynamics of Vermont. Bernie&nbsp;
Sanders appeared on CNN to talk about I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.100
he I don't know that he appeared to talk about&nbsp;
Kirsten cinema, but Dana Bash certainly talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.860
to him about it and he was pretty clear he's&nbsp;
not super impressed with Kirsten. Cinema calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:52.380
her a corporate Democrat and says quite a&nbsp;
few accurate but critical things about her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.880
Let's take a listen to what Bernie had to say.
Speaker 2: You are an independent. She is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:03.000
the third official independent of the US Senate&nbsp;
joining you and Angus King of Maine. What do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.700
think of her decision and also what you just&nbsp;
heard in her interview with Jake? I don't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:15.360
to spend a whole lot of time on Senator Cinema.&nbsp;
She has her reasons. Donna I happen to suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:22.140
that it's probably a lot to do with politics back&nbsp;
in Arizona. I think the Democrats there are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.060
all that enthusiastic about somebody who helped&nbsp;
sabotage some of the most important legislation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.220
that protects the interests of working families.
Speaker 1: And so that's that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.180
pretty clear shot across the bow.
Speaker 2: Voting rights and so forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.560
So I think it really has to do with her political&nbsp;
aspirations for the future in Arizona. But for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:49.320
I think nothing much has changed in terms of&nbsp;
the functioning of the US Senate. The good news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.360
is that we now have our 51 votes. We'll have a&nbsp;
majority on committees. That means that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.220
